[269.478468, "o", "1:## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence\r\n7:greatest `effective` date wins. For each winning notice, `previous_value` in\r\n9:applies (after ledger, amendments, and emails). Owner corrections override\r\n12:## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence — field notes\r\n14:Site visit #195 to corporate accounting captured mailbox exports showing how intercompany netting dispute correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n24:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n26:Lead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corporate accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving"]
[269.480451, "o", " after batch lock.\r\n28:In April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n30:In April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n34:The committee packet references intercompany netting dispute as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n36:Lead reviewer S. Patel noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corporate accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n38:In April settlement, exter"]
[269.480508, "o", "nal advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n40:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n42:Site visit #732 to corporate accounting captured mailbox exports showing how intercompany netting dispute correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n44:Risk assessment WP-3639 ties intercompany netting dispute to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n46:Lead reviewer S. Patel noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corpora"]
[269.480551, "o", "te accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n48:In April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n50:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n52:Site visit #319 to corporate accounting captured mailbox exports showing how intercompany netting dispute correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n54:Memo WP-1664 summarizes stakeholder interviews about intercompany netting dispute. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendment"]
[269.480592, "o", "s should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n56:Risk assessment WP-5948 ties intercompany netting dispute to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n60:Lead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corporate accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n62:Site visit #512 to corporate accounting captured mailbox exports showing how intercompany netting dispute correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n64:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersede"]
[269.480631, "o", "s that draft practice.\r\n66:The committee packet references intercompany netting dispute as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n68:Lead reviewer R. Okonkwo noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corporate accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n72:Follow-up #812 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n74:Follow-up #906 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n==== /tmp/brief09.md ====\r\n"]
[269.484951, "o", "5:Mid-year QA found compliance holds disappearing after date corrections. This\r\n6:amendment supersedes any earlier wording about hold timing. For `compliance_hold`\r\n11:mail-driven status changes; this amendment governs only the hold flag.\r\n17:Lead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n19:Risk assessment WP-7766 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n21:Follow-up #977 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n23:Lead reviewer M. Chen noted that capital project capitalization created reconcili"]
[269.485, "o", "ation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n25:Site visit #872 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n27:Risk assessment WP-9137 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n29:Lead reviewer A. Ndiaye noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n31:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. Th"]
[269.485017, "o", "e mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n33:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n37:Site visit #975 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n39:Risk assessment WP-4185 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n41:Memo WP-2693 summarizes stakeholder interviews about capital project capitalization. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n43:Lead reviewer M. Chen"]
[269.485028, "o", " noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n45:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n47:Lead reviewer L. Bergstrom noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n49:In July review, external advisors reviewed capital project capitalization and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n51:The committee packet references capital project capitalization as a co"]
[269.485044, "o", "ntributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n53:Follow-up #775 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n57:Risk assessment WP-6250 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n59:Working paper WP-1770 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n61:Lead reviewer M. Chen noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overri"]
[269.485056, "o", "des arriving after batch lock.\r\n63:Site visit #134 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n65:Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n67:Memo WP-1725 summarizes stakeholder interviews about capital project capitalization. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n69:Site visit #521 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n71:The committee packet references capi"]
[269.485068, "o", "tal project capitalization as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n75:Site visit #638 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n"]
